>> title: Clear Chicken Broth, Instant Pot
>> category: Soups
>> tags: chicken, broth, instant pot, pressure cooker, deli, make-ahead
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot chicken broth, pressure cooker chicken stock, instant pot chicken stock, pressure cooker chicken broth, quick chicken soup, jewish penicillin, soup base
>> servings: 8
>> dish: chicken-broth
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> pairs-with: matzo-ball-soup, schmaltz, chicken-noodle-soup
>> time: 1 hr 50 min
>> slack: forgiving — a sealed pot cannot be skimmed, so it is all decided before the lid closes; strain without pressing or it comes out cloudy
>> step.1: cover with cold water, aromatics in, lock the lid
>> step.2: cook at high pressure 45 min
>> step.3: wait out the natural release 25 min, strain without pressing
>> step.4: stand 20 min, lift the fat, salt to taste

Cover @chicken backs and wings{3%lb}(1.35 kg; a whole boiling fowl is better if you can find one) with @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L) in the #Instant Pot{} and put @yellow onion{2%large}(halved, skin on for colour), @carrots{3}, @celery{3%ribs}, @parsnip{1}, @fresh dill{1/2%bunch}, @flat-leaf parsley{1/2%bunch}, @bay leaf{1} and @black peppercorns{1%tsp} in with them. Everything goes in cold and everything goes in at once — there is no skimming a sealed pot, and no lifting the vegetables out early either, so the whole recipe is decided before the lid goes on.

Lock the lid on @&(~1)pot{} and ~pressure cook{45%min} at high pressure. Forty-five minutes is the figure every tested pressure-cooker stock lands on; it is not three hours divided by anything, and pushing it to two hours gets you bone broth, which is a different thing with a different mouthfeel.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{25%min} until the pin drops, then strain through a fine sieve without pressing on the solids. Let it come down on its own: cracking the valve boils the broth hard inside the pot and clouds what you are about to strain. Pressing does the same damage by hand.

Let @&(~1)strained broth{} ~stand{20%min} in a tall jug until the fat rises in a clear yellow layer, then spoon it off — or pour the lot through a fat separator, which is quicker and cleaner. That is schmaltz and belongs in a jar. Season with @kosher salt{1%Tbs}(to taste). Against the eleven-and-a-half-hour version this is a shade cloudier and it never sets to the same firm wobble, because forty-five minutes pulls less collagen than three hours; it is gold, it smells like Sunday, and it is ready before lunch. If you want the wobble, chill it overnight anyway and lift a proper cap off in the morning.
